list.item:
Anderson,
                            Robert B., Secretary of the Navy from February 4, 1953;
                        Deputy Secretary of Defense from May 3, 1954.

Briggs, Ellis
                            O., Ambassador in Czechoslovakia to August 27, 1952;
                        Ambassador in Korea from August 25, 1952.

Dulles, John
                            Foster, Consultant to the Secretary of State to April 1952;
                        Secretary of State from January 21, 1953.

Matthews, H.
                            Freeman, Deputy Under Secretary of State to October 11, 1953;
                        Ambassador in the Netherlands after November 25, 1953.

Nehru,
                            R.K., Indian Secretary for United Nations Affairs from 1952;
                        Indian Secretary for External Affairs from July 1953.

Nixon, Richard
                            M., Republican Senator from California to January 1953;
                        thereafter, Vice President of the United States.

Pearson,
                            Lester B., Canadian Secretary of State for External Affairs;
                        Chairman of the Canadian Delegation at the Seventh, Eighth, and Ninth
                        Regular Sessions of the United Nations General Assembly, 1952-1953-1954;
                        President of the Seventh Regular Session, 1952.

Pyun Yung
                            Tai, Minister of Foreign Affairs, Republic of Korea;
                        Prime Minister from June 1954.

Smith, Walter
                            Bedell, Director of Central Intelligence to February 8, 1953;
                        Under Secretary of State, February 9, 1953–October 1, 1954.

White,
                            Lieutenant General Thomas D., USAF, Deputy Chief of Air Force Staff (Operations), to 1953;
                        thereafter, Vice Chief of Staff.

person.note:
Deputy Assistant Secretary of State for European Affairs to April 1954;
                    thereafter Special Assistant to the Permanent Representative in Europe at
                    Paris.

Director of the Bureau of German Affairs, Department of State, to 1952;
                    Assistant Secretary of State for Near Eastern, South Asian, and African Affairs
                    from April 14, 1952.

Chief, Division of Research for the Far East, Department of State, to 1954;
                    First Secretary and Consul of the Embassy in the Philippines from March 25,
                    1954; Counselor of Embassy from October 22, 1954.

Deputy Assistant Secretary of State for Far Eastern Affairs to October 1953;
                    Ambassador in Czechoslovakia from December 1953; United States Coordinator for
                    the Geneva Conference, 1954.

Assistant Secretary of State for Congressional Relations to September 9, 1952;
                    Minister in Finland, November 15, 1952–May 1, 1953; thereafter,
                    Ambassador.
